# Krishanu Kaundilya Personal Portfolio — Project Rules & Standards

## 1. Mandatory Phase-Wise Development & Checkpoints
- Development is strictly structured across sequential phases (Phase 0 through Phase 6).
- **Rule:** Never jump ahead or build components belonging to future phases.
- **Rule:** Never generate the entire website in one single blind operation.
- **Rule:** After completing each phase, update `roadmap.md`, `docs/CHANGELOG.md`, and `docs/DEVELOPER_GUIDE.md` (if applicable).
- **Rule:** Run linting (`npm run lint`) and TypeScript checks (`npx tsc --noEmit`), and report actual outcomes honestly.
- **Approval Checkpoint:** Stop and wait for the user's explicit approval before proceeding to the next phase.

## 2. Component Reuse & Prevention of Duplication
- **Inspect Before Creating:** Always inspect `src/components/` before creating a new component. If an existing component performs the same or similar role, reuse or extend it via props and composition.
- **Shared Primitives:**
  - One shared `Navbar` with responsive mobile drawer.
  - One shared `Footer` with social and navigational links.
  - One shared `Button` with variants (`primary`, `secondary`, `outline`, `pill-cta`).
  - One shared `Badge` / `Pill` component for chips and tags.
  - One shared `SectionHeading` for consistent typographic rhythm and badges across sections.
  - One shared `Container` for layout width and responsive padding constraints.
  - Reusable card primitives for Experience, Achievements, Services, and Testimonials.
- Do not duplicate JSX structures, styling classes, event listeners, or layout wrappers across different page sections.

## 3. DRY, Separation of Concerns & Single Responsibility
- **Data-Driven Content:** All profile information (bio, roles, experiences, credentials, achievements, services, testimonials) must be centralized in typed data files under `src/data/` (e.g., `portfolioData.ts`).
- Presentation components must be pure or focused on layout/interaction, receiving data via typed props.
- Never hardcode raw copy, lists, or company metrics directly inside deep JSX blocks.
- Extract utility functions (date formatting, class combining) into `src/lib/utils.ts`.

## 4. Professional Visual Design & Brand Alignment
- The site must reflect a senior cybersecurity professional, AI agent architect, and startup founder.
- Avoid generic AI templates, repetitive identical card grids, excessive glowing neon effects, or meaningless decorative shapes.
- **Design Tokens & Palette:**
  - **Background:** Warm off-white / linen (`#F9F8F4` / `#FAF8F5`) with a subtle grid pattern overlay.
  - **Navbar:** Floating dark pill container (`#1E1E1E` / `#232323`), subtle border, high blur.
  - **Accent & CTAs:** Warm amber / golden orange (`#F59E0B` / `#D97706` / `#E58A1F`) inspired by CoreDhristi branding.
  - **Text:** Dark charcoal (`#171717`) for primary copy, muted slate (`#666666`) for secondary details.
  - **Hero Media Card:** Dark rounded container showcasing the verified portrait (`public/Images/Sir image.jpg`), accompanied by floating metric badges (`7-Layer Security`, `Solutions for the AI Agent Economy`).
- Follow the visual references provided (screenshot from `localhost:3000` and the user's wireframe).

## 5. Responsive Behavior & Accessibility (WCAG AA)
- Test layouts across mobile (360px - 480px), tablet (768px), laptop (1024px), and desktop (1280px+).
- Ensure no horizontal overflow (`overflow-x: hidden` safeguards where appropriate).
- Proper heading hierarchy: exactly one `<h1>` per page, followed by logical `<h2>` and `<h3>` tags.
- All interactive controls (`<button>`, `<a>`) must have accessible names, visible focus outlines, and keyboard navigation support (`tabIndex`, `Enter`, `Space`, `Escape` for drawers/modals).
- Meaningful `alt` text for informative images (e.g., portrait photo of Krishanu Kaundilya). Empty `alt=""` for purely decorative icons/illustrations.
- Ensure color contrast ratios meet or exceed WCAG 2.1 AA standards (minimum 4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text).

## 6. Next.js 16 & React 19 Architecture Best Practices
- Utilize the Next.js App Router (`src/app/`).
- Leverage React Server Components (RSC) by default. Add `"use client"` directive only to components requiring browser events, state, or hooks (e.g., mobile menu toggle, interactive tabs, back-to-top).
- Use `next/image` with proper `priority`, `sizes`, `width`, `height`, and responsive styling to eliminate layout shifts (CLS).
- Keep dependencies minimal. Rely on built-in Tailwind CSS v4, Lucide icons (or clean SVG icons), and standard Web APIs. Do not install heavy or redundant third-party libraries.

## 7. Truthful Content & Zero Hallucination
- Use only verified professional credentials, dates, and milestones for Krishanu Kaundilya:
  - Founder @ CoreDhristi (incorporation: CoreDhristi LLP, Oct 2026).
  - Background in Cybersecurity (VAPT, SAST/DAST, offensive security, CEH Master, custom security tooling).
  - AI Agent & Automation developer (7-layer review stack: Betterleaks, Semgrep, CodeQL, OSV-Scanner, QA, Promptfoo, Agentic Radar, MCP Inspector, Presidio, Syft).
